.resources/TASK-260715-1tnjlu/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_accepted-inputs.md:1:Architecture-decision brief from the active macOS-first goal. Treat TASK-260715-1tnjlu as the critical DNS policy entry point. Read .spec/architecture.md, .spec/security-privacy.md, .spec/relay-protocol.md, .spec/validation.md, .spec/decisions.md, the accepted M1 runtime/profile contracts, and relevant board dependencies. Compare explicit per-profile resolver endpoints, an accountable documented product default, exit-host discovery, and tunneled DoH on privacy, bootstrap circularity, IPv4/IPv6 reachability from the exit, UDP/TCP semantics, DNSSEC transparency, operator expectations, migration, M4 UI, M2 relay/degraded compatibility, testability, and operational cost. Preserve fail-closed DNS: after routes apply ordinary queries must never touch or silently fall back to the physical resolver; no remote shell parsing without a separately approved bounded security design; no fake DNS; no public resolver vendor selected by taste. Freeze exact schema fields, validation, address-family behavior, port, transport, timeout/failure semantics, migration and downstream task impacts only when derivable from accepted evidence. Numeric tuning remains injectable where the goal makes it an evidence gate. Cite concrete primary/project evidence and concrete downstream task IDs. An independent Codex reviewer may approve an architecture policy derived entirely from existing accepted invariants; if a genuinely accountable product choice remains that cannot be derived, attach a complete options/tradeoffs/recommendation packet, mark blocked, and request only the exact choice. Persist the decision in a task-scoped outcome and the project source of truth, run docs/spec/board consistency checks, and hand off to review.
